.TH V32DEV 3v32 "October 15, 2026" "v32dev 20261014-dev" "Vircon32 C Library"
.SH NAME
v32dev \- files, a terminal and other devices for Vircon32 programs, through the memory card
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B #include \(dqstorage.h\(dq
.B #include \(dqserial.h\(dq
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
A Vircon32 program can only reach what the console has: video, sound, gamepads
and a memory card. The v32dev project uses the memory card to give a program a
way to exchange data with the computer that runs the emulator: read and write
a file, talk to a terminal, and later use a network. This page is the overview
of the C library a program uses for that. It has three headers:
.TP
.BR v32dev.h (3v32)
The core. Finds out whether the memory card carries a live mailbox and
whether a host is serving a channel of it, and moves words or messages through
the channel. Knows nothing of what the data means. Included by the other two.
.TP
.BR storage.h (3v32)
.B storage
\(em a block device: a file of the host, read and written in sectors, or by
word at any position. The way to load data into, and save data out of, the
emulator.
.TP
.BR serial.h (3v32)
.B serial
\(em a character device: a stream of words in both directions, such as the
terminal that runs the host tool.
.PP
Every function, structure and constant has its own page that opens the page of
the header it belongs to, so
.B "man storage_read"
and
.B "man V32DEV_READY"
work.
.SS How it works
The last 64 KiB of the memory card are a
.IR mailbox :
four
.I channels
that each hold a ring of words from the host to the program, a ring from the
program to the host, and some control words. The program only reads and writes
card memory; no input or output ports are involved. A patched emulator keeps
the mailbox synchronized with the card file on disk, and a
.I host
program (the
.B v32dev.py
tool of the project) reads and writes that file and decides what is at the
other end of each channel, in the manner of unix devices: a
.B block
device (a disk), a
.B char
device (a terminal), a
.B pipe
or a
.B socket.
The host announces the class and kind of the device on a channel, and a program
says which class it expects.
.PP
Because the host only sees what the program wrote at the end of a frame, and
the program only sees what the host wrote at the start of one, a request and
its reply take at least one frame. The programs of this library therefore
either wait by ending frames, or let the program go on drawing and look again
on the next frame.
.PP
The complete description of the layout and of the protocol is in the file
.I PROTOCOL.md
of the project documents.
.SS Setting up
The card is prepared on the host before the emulator loads it, and the host
serves the devices while the program runs:
.PP
.nf
v32dev.py format game.memc \-\-ch0 storage \-\-ch1 serial
v32dev.py serve  game.memc \-\-ch0 storage:notes.txt,bytes \-\-ch1 serial:stdio
.fi
.PP
Then run the program in an emulator that has the v32dev patch, with
.I game.memc
as its memory card. The host can be started before or after the program, and
restarted while it runs. Without the patch the program still works: its
channels report that there is no live mailbox.
.SS Programs
A program creates a device on a channel (0 to 3), calls its
.B probe
function once on every frame (or uses the functions that wait, which do it),
and then uses it:
.PP
.RS
.nf
#include "video.h"
#include "storage.h"

void main()
{
    int [128] words;
    storage* disk = storage_init( 0 );

    if( storage_wait_ready( &disk, 600 ) )
    {
        storage_read_words( &disk, 0, words, 128 );
        storage_write_words( &disk, 0, words, 128 );
        storage_flush( &disk );
    }

    storage_free( &disk );

    while( true )
    {
        end_frame();
    }
}
.fi
.RE
.PP
There is nothing to link. The headers are installed beside the Vircon32
DevTools headers, so
.BR compile (1)
finds them with a plain
.B #include \(dqstorage.h\(dq
and no options, and it compiles only the functions that the program calls.
.SS Where things are
After the project is installed (see the
.I README
of the project):
.TP
.I Vircon32/DevTools/include
The headers, in the directory where the DevTools put theirs.
.TP
.I /usr/local/share/man/man3
These manual pages.
.TP
.I Vircon32/v32tools/docs/v32dev
The documentation of the project, the protocol description and the example
program.
.PP
The host tool and the emulator patch are not installed by the headers: they
are in the
.I tools
and
.I emulator/v32dev
directories of the project.
.SS Related
.B v32io
is the sibling of v32dev in the same project. It carries a keyboard and a mouse
on a gamepad port, works on real hardware, and needs no emulator patch.
Its manual, if installed, starts at
.B man v32io.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR v32dev.h (3v32),
.BR storage.h (3v32),
.BR serial.h (3v32),
.BR vircon32 (3v32),
.BR memcard.h (3v32),
.BR compile (1)
.SH AUTHOR
Matthew Haas, for the v32io project. The Vircon32 standard library it builds
on was written by Javier Carracedo
.RI ( Carra ).
